055904 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "73b8ac60-1c84-5c86-a80d-cef53c382fb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055904 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055927 #edb/ref 1064 "We're now able to prove the following result:\n\n![](images/43/439752d1d1d2d58f72839646de282cb987a26fe34e64831609b41a82dc1527cb.svg)\n\nFirst, we set \n\n![](images/f3/f3ef524df5ab32cde6c5682bdd188ad71e2d7c5e17e33924c4922690c9a1e3d1.svg) \nOur goal is to find ![](images/c7/c7244a31f756199a29c0e7e755eccdf9274346e643e0856566469b35a207e53a.svg)\n\nFirst, note that since ![](images/79/7985253d838299238e3faf6ec581b3b9dbe915a85722df9603c3aa11fc6e2990.svg) we have\n\n![](images/4e/4e736e470bb01bf341195a833a172b7284cd6d5284f2256fa149be409f043901.svg)\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg) we get the following:\n\n![](images/9e/9eae12637052db2e76d66bb39a24492924a69827b818cae0d47b8a881adea1d6.svg)\n\nFinally, since ![](images/c7/c7ac0127049c8701655a3f89e3698cee38c3fa26e3cdbc7161c1de3dc11a862c.svg) we conclude\n\n![](images/2f/2fba7b56204f6f63d0b1edcb20567c44c17144f94e4efb4803ab9a1f3e43805f.svg)\nas required." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055927 #edb/ref 1065 10531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055927 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "703b0ae9-246b-57d0-a0af-5bee4dbafe4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055927 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Formula for the Derivative of the Natural Logarithm" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055933 #edb/ref 1064 "When simplifying numerical expressions, it is generally preferable to avoid having radicals in the denominator. In cases where we wish to remove a radical from a denominator, we must **rationalize the denominator**.\n\nIf the denominator of an expression consists of two terms and includes one or more radicals, we rationalize it by multiplying both the numerator and the denominator by the **conjugate** of the denominator.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1+\\sqrt{2}}\n$$\n\nTo find the conjugate of the denominator $1+\\sqrt{2}$, we *flip the sign* between the two terms. So, the conjugate of the denominator is\n\n$$\n1\\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we start by multiplying both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $1-\\sqrt{2}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\sqrt{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{2}}{1 - \\sqrt{2}} &=  \\\\\n(1 \\cdot (1 - \\sqrt{2}))/((1 + \\sqrt{2})(1 - \\sqrt{2})) &=  \\\\\n(1 - \\sqrt{2})/((1 + \\sqrt{2})(1 - \\sqrt{2})) &= \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)(a-b) = a^2 - b^2\n$$\n\nApplying the difference of squares formula with\n\n$$\na=1, b=\\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nto simplify the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 - \\sqrt{2})/((1 + \\sqrt{2})(1 - \\sqrt{2})) &=  \\\\\n(1 - \\sqrt{2})/(1^{2} - (\\sqrt{2})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1 - \\sqrt{2}}{1 - 2} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1 - \\sqrt{2}}{-1} &=  \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055933 #edb/ref 1065 13463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055933 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2b34f4d4-8931-5c24-8872-dfa9420a1a87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055933 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055959 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ in the first quadrant, measured in the usual way.\n\n![](images/3d/3d51ca417ba4f6f62bd3e5db5997842d0671f18920a7de70578146a49de79ec3.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the $x$-coordinate of $P$ coincides with the cosine of the angle $\\theta$, and\n- the $y$-coordinate coincides with the sine of the angle $\\theta$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nConsequently, if we know the angle $\\theta$, we can easily work out the coordinates of the point $P$, and vice-versa. This works regardless of whether the angle $\\theta$ is given in degrees or radians.\n\nFor now, we'll focus on points located in the first quadrant. Later, we will encounter points in all quadrants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055959 #edb/ref 1065 2493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055959 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8e9912a6-a3c6-5076-9724-a53f5e215dd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055959 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055960 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the unit circle and a point $P(x,y)$ that lies on the circle.\n\n![](images/3d/3d51ca417ba4f6f62bd3e5db5997842d0671f18920a7de70578146a49de79ec3.png)\n\nTo prove that\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nlet's examine the triangle that's formed by $\\overline{OP}$ and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/53/53d84bedcddcb484bc613a2f01e002afe16b614daa909ff744195bcc45464472.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The hypotenuse has length $1$ since it is a radius of the unit circle.\n- The adjacent and opposite sides have lengths $x$ and $y$, respectively.\n\nTherefore, using the definitions of $\\cos\\theta$ and $\\sin\\theta$, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{x}{1} = x, \\qquad \\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{1} = y\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055960 #edb/ref 1065 2494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055960 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e7838544-050b-5270-a16a-1679e3489a02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055960 #edb/ref 1067 "A Proof of the Unit Circle Property" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055963 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the tangent of an angle using the unit circle, we need to use the relationship\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nTo see why this is true, let's take a look at the following triangle.\n\n![](images/99/99b51d7d25a266dbfda7839be6bafc335b181ecd6dc74224fcb0557180f872aa.png)\n\nFor this particular triangle, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{z}, \\qquad \\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{x}{z}, \\qquad \\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nWhen we take $\\sin\\theta$ and divide it by $\\cos\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sin θ}{\\cos θ} &= \\frac{y}{z} \\div \\frac{x}{z} \\\\\n&= \\frac{y}{z} \\cdot \\frac{z}{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{y \\cdot z}{x \\cdot z} \\\\\n&= \\frac{y \\cdot z}{x \\cdot z} \\\\\n&= \\frac{y}{x} \\\\\n&= \\tan θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough this proof is only for acute angles, the identity is true for any angle, provided that $\\tan\\theta$ is well-defined.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan\\theta}\n$$\n\nit immediately follows that\n\n$$\n\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\\right)} = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055963 #edb/ref 1065 2725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055963 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a17ae159-de66-547d-8e9a-2190b512ab3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055963 #edb/ref 1067 "The Relationship Between Sine, Cosine, and Tangent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055964 #edb/ref 1064 "It's possible to perform arithmetic with functions, just like with numbers. Shown below are the four arithmetic operations, addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\cdot)$, and division $(/)$, applied to the functions\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4x + 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = x - 2\n$$\n\n- **Addition**\n$\\begin{aligned} (f + g)(x) &= f(x) + g(x) \\\\ &= (4x + 1) + (x - 2) \\\\ &= 4x + 1 + x - 2 \\\\ &= 5x - 1 \\end{aligned}$\n- **Subtraction**\n$(f - g)(x)|= f(x) - g(x); = (4x + 1) - (x - 2); = 4x + 1 - x + 2; = 3x + 3$\n- **Multiplication**\n$(fg)(x)|= f(x)g(x); = (4x + 1)(x - 2); = 4x^{2} - 8x + x - 2; = 4x^{2} - 7x - 2$\n- **Division**\n$(f/g)(x)|= (f(x))/(g(x)); = \\frac{4x + 1}{x - 2}$\n\n**Note:** In the case of division, we must have that\n\n$$\ng(x)\\neq 0\n$$\n\nso that we avoid dividing by zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055964 #edb/ref 1065 2751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055964 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c656d962-19ad-5e04-b117-fa8d0c9034b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055964 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055971 #edb/ref 1064 "When we're asked to evaluate the sum of two functions at a particular point, there are two methods that we can use. We could either evaluate both functions at the point and sum the results. Or, we could sum the two functions and then evaluate the result at the point.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the $(f+g)(1)$ for\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2 -1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = 2x-3\n$$\n\nThe notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f + g)(1)$, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1)$, and then add the results:\n\nFirst, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= (1)^{2} - 1 = 0 \\\\\ng(1) &= 2(1) - 3 =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(1) &= f(1) + g(1) \\\\\n&= 0 + (-1) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(x) &= (x^{2} - 1) + (2x - 3) \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2x - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f + g)(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(1) &= (1)^{2} + 2(1) - 4 =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth these methods work fine, but method $1$ is usually faster.\n\nFinally, note that we can evaluate the difference between two functions using the same methods." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055971 #edb/ref 1065 2834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055971 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "74671b66-6f9b-59a5-96e2-aeb7ca503afe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055971 #edb/ref 1067 "Evaluating the Sum or Difference of Two Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055989 #edb/ref 1064 "Functions are usually given in the form ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) where ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) is explicitly written as a function of ![](images/cf/cff7b257e3c71ec70dc7cd273bdd1cced4254993a43cffbfc338460c21bc7319.svg) However, they can also be written in **implicit** form, like\n![](images/82/82728d8a7de8d66f1952d6404473ba2e8130c13c9536b2d335b05684c9e9b382.svg)\n\nWhen a function is given in implicit form, we can differentiate it using the method of **implicit differentiation**, which consists of the following two steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to ![](images/cf/cff7b257e3c71ec70dc7cd273bdd1cced4254993a43cffbfc338460c21bc7319.svg)\n\nWe need to remember that ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) *is a function of* ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg) i.e., ![](images/6f/6f3c9a64baef025798baeebc2d92c4361ec210293ebf0b0b5c64055541a50ae3.svg) So, we apply the chain rule:\n![](images/7d/7d48f6e8d69bab060f3e0a41927e97481d0a8f05b424b8bdb5639dfcc58fbc76.svg)\n\n**Step 2**: Solve the equation for ![](images/c7/c7244a31f756199a29c0e7e755eccdf9274346e643e0856566469b35a207e53a.svg)\n![](images/80/80fd42d93eb7ab2cce44347268e404853f4cf507896d8097af70a92323962377.svg)\n\nSo, the derivative of ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) is ![](images/42/420cc2cbdc995a548ee077e5547ca1522bd2c1cdbbd31f304a4466497acacb5c.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055989 #edb/ref 1065 433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055989 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4f8a0764-c99e-5be6-8b49-3ca28725caf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055989 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056004 #edb/ref 1064 "A **mathematical statement** is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either **true** or **false**.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The sentence, \"The sum of $1$ and $2$ is $3$\" is a mathematical statement because it is *true.*\n\n- Likewise, \"$2+2=5$\" is a mathematical statement because it is *false.*\n\nThe following examples are *not* mathematical statements:\n\n- \"Find the sum of $1$ and $2$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false. It is an instruction.\n\n- \"What is the sum of $1$ and $2?$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false. It is a question.\n\n- Similarly, \"$6+9$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\nWe often use upper-case letters such as $P, Q,$ and $R$ to represent statements. For example:\n\n- $P:$ The sum of two even integers is odd.\n\n- $Q: 3 \\in \\mathbb Z$\n\n- $R: \\{2,4,6\\ldots\\}\\cup \\{1,3,5,\\ldots\\} = \\mathbb N$\n\nIn this case, the statements $Q$ and $R$ are true, whereas statement $P$ is false.\n\nWe often refer to the **truth value** of a mathematical statement. We use [math]T[/math] to denote \"true\" and [math]F[/math] to denote \"false.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056004 #edb/ref 1065 7961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056004 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "27451355-1bea-50f9-8b12-71af26b849e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056004 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056051 #edb/ref 1064 "A **predicate** (or **open sentence**) of one variable is a sentence that contains a variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute a concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a mathematical statement that is either *true* or *false*.\n\nFor example, consider the following predicate:\n\n> $x > 1$\n\nThis is a predicate because substituting concrete values for $x$ gives a mathematical statement.\n\nFor instance: \n\n- Substituting $x=2$ gives the *true* statement \"$2 > 1$\"\n\n- Substituting $x=5$ gives the *true* statement \"$5 > 1$\"\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ gives the *false* statement \"$0 > 1$\"\n\nWe denote predicates using capital letters and the corresponding variable in parentheses. In this example, we can write our predicate more formally as follows:\n\n> $P(x):$ $x > 1$\n\nAny predicate must be defined with its **universal set,** which is the set from which we are allowed to pull the concrete values of the variable. So, we could state our predicate more formally as\n\n> $P(x):$ $x > 1,$ $\\quad$ where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nThe universal set can often be identified from the context and doesn't need to be explicitly stated. For example, if we have the predicate \n\n> $n$ is divisible by 5,\n\nwe can assume that $n$ is defined on the set of all integers, i.e., $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$\n\nIn math, *any equation or inequality is a predicate*. However, a sentence containing a variable is not necessarily a predicate. For example, the sentence \n\n$\\qquad$ \"What is the sign of $y?$\"\n\nis *not* a predicate because substituting a concrete value for $y$ does not give a mathematical statement. Instead, it gives a question. \n\nSimilarly, \n\n> $z^2-5$\n\nis *not* a predicate either. It's just an expression." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056051 #edb/ref 1065 8303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056051 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6162ecd9-50ee-52a9-9651-ba95a586ee9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056051 #edb/ref 1067 "Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056074 #edb/ref 1064 "Before we continue, we need a brief discussion on the concept of **integer divisibility.**\n\nSuppose $a$ and $b$ are integers. We write $a  \\mid b$ to mean that $\\boldsymbol a$ **divides** $\\boldsymbol b.$ Informally, this means the quotient of $\\dfrac b a$ is an *integer*, and there is *no remainder*. \n\nFor example, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n5 \\,|\\, 20\n$$\n\nThis statement reads, \"$5$ divides $20$\". Moreover, this is a *true* statement because $20\\div 5 = 4$ is an integer. \n\nNow consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n6\\,  |\\, 20\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false* because $20\\div 6 = 3 {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac13}},$ which is *not* an integer. The correct statement is\n\n$$\n6 \\! \\not | \\: 20.\n$$\n\nThis statement reads, \"$6$ does not divide $20$\".\n\nFor now, this is all we need to understand the notation $a\\mid b.$ More formal discussions on integer divisibility will be given in future lessons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056074 #edb/ref 1065 9348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056074 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ac10b43c-74e8-5752-9d06-2d746c31cb0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056074 #edb/ref 1067 "Divisibility Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059651 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059651 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059651 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059651 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059651 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059651 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059651 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059651 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059651 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059651 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Rational Expressions Using an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059651 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059651 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "23637774-8d69-500d-8cb0-5e41a0207542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059652 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059652 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059652 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059652 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059652 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059652 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059652 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059652 #e